  {"type": "split", "reverse": True, "h2": "240V, panel capacity and what the electrician checks", "image": "Diagram of a Midea EVOX G3 heat pump and air handler serving a two-storey home", "image_src": "/assets/img/brands/midea-evox-g3-system.webp", "image_fit": "contain", "image_pos": "50% 50%", "image_credit": "Image: Midea", "html": "<p>An all-electric system needs two things from your panel: a dedicated 240V circuit for the outdoor heat pump, and a second, larger 240V circuit for the air handler's heat strips. Together they're the biggest load in the house. Our ESA-licensed electricians do a load calculation on the assessment visit, count what's coming out (baseboards, an electric furnace, an old AC) against what's going in, and tell you plainly whether the existing panel carries it.</p><p>Most homes that already heat with electricity are fine. Homes coming off oil or propane with an older panel sometimes need an upgrade, and we quote that up front in the guaranteed price, not as a surprise on install day.</p>"},
  {"type": "text", "h2": "Running cost: heat pump and air handler versus baseboards", "html": "<p>Baseboards and electric furnaces are 100% efficient, which sounds good until you remember a heat pump is 200% to 300% efficient on the same hydro. For every dollar of electricity, a cold-climate heat pump delivers two to three dollars' worth of heat across an Ontario heating season, even counting the hours the strips run in deep cold. The exact saving depends on your heat loss, your region and how often the strips run, so we show you the numbers for your house rather than a national average.</p><p>Two things keep the savings real: sizing from a heat-loss calculation so the heat pump carries the house down to its rated -15°C capacity, and setting the thermostat's auxiliary heat lockout correctly. Both are standard on every EHI install.</p>"},
